add('Meet the GitHub repository',None,[('strong a','Udemy-Claude-Agent-SDK-Student-Rebuild','This is the actual GitHub repository for the Claude Agent SDK student learning platform. You can browse its files before installing anything. Check that the owner is luiscoco and that the repository name ends with Student Rebuild.'),('a','56fc40f','This recording uses the documentation update shown here. GitHub will continue to evolve, so your latest commit can differ. The README is the starting point for setup and the current scope.')])
add('Choose your route through the README','Start here',[('tr','Run the supplied application','The Start here table gives you several routes. Choose Quick start to run the supplied application, or Run your first lesson for a guided first exercise. These links jump directly to the relevant README section.'),('tr','Rebuild the platform yourself','If you want to build the platform yourself, open Student build materials. Repository map explains the implementation folders, and Verification lists the checks. Release scope and the preserved history answer more detailed questions.')])
add('Clone, install and start','Quick start',[('p','Install Node.js 24.x','Begin with Node twenty-four and Git. The engine declaration excludes Node twenty-five and above. Check the versions in a new terminal before installing dependencies.'),('pre','git clone','Clone the repository, enter its directory, and run npm ci with include optional. This reproduces the committed lockfile. Then run npm run dev to start the platform. These are instructions displayed in the repository; this capture is not a terminal installation recording.'),('p','Open http://localhost:5180/','Open localhost on port fifty-one eighty after the frontend-ready message. Prepared exercises do not require an API key, so you can begin exploring without authorizing a model request.')])
add('Understand the two local processes','Student frontend',[('tr','Student frontend','The browser interface uses port fifty-one eighty by default. The local Express host uses thirty seventy-seven. The student web and API port settings let you change the pair when necessary.'),('p','npm run dev starts both','The default development command builds complete frontend bundles, watches changes, and serves Vite preview. Completed rebuilds reload the page. A separate dev HMR command provides the alternative hot-module workflow.'),('p','Vite proxies','Vite forwards API requests to the local host. Keep both port settings consistent. Strict ports report collisions instead of silently opening a different course instance. Stop your own development terminal before restarting.')])
add('Run your first prepared lesson','Run your first lesson',[('ol','Open the course home','Open Lesson one, read its guide, and inspect the concept map or walkthrough. In Run the exercise, keep the prepared scenario selected. Run it, inspect the streamed observations and final result, then open TypeScript source to connect the behavior to code.'),('p','Prepared fixtures emit','Prepared observations are authored fixtures. They can still perform owned local host actions. They are not live model responses, and a passing prepared check does not establish that a paid provider call succeeded. Keep automatic evidence and your manual review separate.')])
add('Authorize live work deliberately','Optional Live Claude exercise',[('ol','Copy .env.example','For optional Live Claude, copy the example configuration to your ignored local environment file, add your own backend key and model, and restart the host. Choose Live Claude in the exercise, inspect the tools and limits, and explicitly authorize one request.'),('p','Keep credentials out','Keep credentials out of the browser, commits, and screenshots. The first query has a one-turn SDK limit and a five-cent SDK budget setting; other lessons differ. A cancellation, error, or partial answer must not be presented as successful completion.')])
add('Explore the learning features','What you can do',[('tr','75 lesson runtimes','The platform contains seventy-five lesson runtimes. You will explore agent queries, messages, tools, permissions, sessions, hooks, MCP connections, and advanced execution surfaces.'),('tr','Guided lesson workspace','The workspace combines guided learning, concept maps, walkthroughs, exercises, and source views. Code excerpts, narrated source tours, and visual assets help connect the concept to a working implementation.'),('tr','AI Tutor','The AI Tutor provides prepared guidance and optional paid answers with fresh consent. Learning records are saved locally. Three milestone starters support cumulative work: a Study Coach, an Agent Evaluation Studio, and an Agent Publishing Studio.')])
add('Know where learning data is saved','Where your data lives',[('ul','Browser storage holds','Supported notes, preferences, and learning records live in browser storage. The host owns local lab files separately. Some lesson registries are memory-only, so persistence depends on the selected runtime.'),('p','This is a loopback-only','Treat this as a local teaching application for a trusted student. It does not provide account authentication, cross-device synchronization, managed backups, or secure multi-user hosting. Preserve the browser data and local files that you need.')])
add('Use the student build materials','Student build materials',[('ol','Materials-only build order','For a student rebuild, follow the materials-only build order, then the student experience prompt sequence. The prompt library includes numbered lessons, shared interface features, the AI Tutor, and milestone stages. Specifications and traceability connect requirements to evidence.'),('p','The supplied teaching guide','The supplied teaching guide and provenance describe the source material. The Word implementation guide is a historical companion. For current navigation and behavior, use the current Markdown contracts and prompts.')])
add('Read the implementation by responsibility','Repository map',[('tr','src/','Start in source for the React interface and browser learning state. The server folder contains Express routes, runtime adapters, permissions, the AI Tutor, and local stores. Shared defines contracts and lesson evidence criteria used across layers.'),('tr','docs/prompts/','Public holds learning assets. Prompts and specifications describe what to build, while validation records describe dated evidence and limitations. Tests and scripts support verification; the specialized folders support milestones, Python, and editor integration.')])
add('Explore the actual lesson source','App.tsx',[('a','App.tsx','Here is the real source directory in GitHub. App dot tsx coordinates the main interface, while the surrounding source files separate learning features and lesson-specific workspaces. Use the file list or Go to file to locate a component before changing it.')],URL+'/tree/main/src')
add('Explore the actual host implementation','app.ts',[('a','app.ts','The server folder contains the host implementation. App dot ts coordinates routes, and runtime dot ts contains the core prepared and live adapters. Lesson-specific modules add their own policies and stores. The browser interface and the agent execution host have different responsibilities.')],URL+'/tree/main/server')
add('Understand the pinned technology baseline','Technology baseline',[('tr','Primary @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk','The primary Agent SDK is pinned to zero point three point three ten. React, Vite, Express, TypeScript, Zod, and Playwright also have explicit versions. Install the lockfile rather than upgrading packages independently while following the course.'),('p','The separate direct API client','The direct Anthropic API client has a different version line. A historical TypeScript alias and the Python lesson also retain separate pins. Read their roles before comparing version numbers.'),('p','The Agent SDK operates','The Agent SDK runs the agent runtime in a process operated by this application. Remote model reasoning is separate from local tool execution. This platform teaches the Agent SDK, rather than the separate Managed Agents hosted-session API.')])
add('Verify your changes','Verification',[('pre','npm run typecheck','After changes, run type checking, the Node tests, and the build. Install Playwright Chromium before the startup and interface checks. The repository provides these commands directly in the README.'),('p','The browser checks start','Browser checks start their own controlled test hosts. Ordinary checks do not authorize paid model calls. Linux and WSL environments may also need browser system libraries.'),('p','For a focused interface change','Choose focused checks for the feature you changed. Paid live runners are separate, explicit actions. Passing an offline test suite does not establish live acceptance of every integration.')])
add('Find help and continue learning','Troubleshooting',[('tr','Port is already in use','Use Troubleshooting when setup fails. Check your Node version, port collisions, build-ready messages, and backend configuration. Stop only your own course processes, and keep your local configuration and progress intact.'),('tr','A scenario needs manual review','A needs-review outcome can be intentional. Read that lesson\'s evidence rule rather than trying to force every scenario to pass. The linked startup repair prompt and validation notes offer deeper guidance.')])
add('Check scope, then begin','Scope and implementation history',[('ul','Current course release scope','Finish by checking current release scope and publication items. Older checkpoints remain available in Development History, along with specialized lesson instructions. Their dates matter; they do not prove every integration has just been retested.'),('p','Release records describe','Your next step is simple: install from the lockfile, run Lesson one in prepared mode, inspect its evidence, and connect it to source. Then follow the lessons and staged projects at your own pace.')])
